<p>A query can return the right number of rows with the wrong values in every one. An unknown charge becomes zero, a division loses its fraction, or a report takes “today” from the wrong clock. None needs a complicated query to go wrong.</p>
<p>We will extend the shop’s order follow-up list from <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a>. Its independent line and payment totals are already checked. Here the task is what each expression means once those totals arrive: which missing facts to preserve, when to round, how to match a label and which instants belong to a reporting day. The syntax and recorded results are <strong>native PostgreSQL 18.6</strong>. The figures replay logical calculations; they do not run a database or show its execution order.</p>

