<section aria-labelledby="start-with-the-answer-each-expression-must-mean">
 <h2 id="start-with-the-answer-each-expression-must-mean">Start with the answer each expression must mean</h2>
 <p>The parent’s candidate stage contains five open January orders. We keep that stage as a small temporary table, <code>order_review</code>, and add a proposed packing fee and an optional display label. This is one row per order, after the parent has combined lines and receipts. It avoids rebuilding the joins while we check the values.</p>
 <p>A missing payment subtotal means no receipts: the underlying receipt amounts cannot be null. A missing order value means the price is not known. A missing fee means nobody has decided it; zero means waived, a positive fee adds a charge and a negative fee gives a credit. The proposed fee may contain a fraction of a currency unit finer than a cent. Our agreed rule rounds the final payable amount to two decimal places, once per order, with halfway values rounded away from zero.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The order expression fixture"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Order value</th><th>Paid subtotal</th><th>Proposed fee</th><th>Preferred label</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>100</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>0</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>101</td><td>30</td><td>15</td><td>1.005</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>102</td><td>20</td><td>NULL</td><td>0</td><td>Empty string</td></tr>
  <tr><td>103</td><td>40</td><td>40</td><td>−2</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>106</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td><td>Edda_100%</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>Before SQL, calculate the expected payables: <code>100 → 0.00</code>, <code>101 → 16.01</code>, <code>102 → 20.00</code>, <code>103 → −2.00</code> and <code>106 → NULL</code>. The follow-up list keeps positive or unknown payables: <strong>101, 102 and 106</strong>, in placement order. Empty or missing preferred labels fall back to the customer’s name; other text is preserved. Expect Mina, Mina and Edda_100%.</p>
 <details><summary>Create the complete fixture in a fresh exercise session</summary><Sql code={setup} label="The parent's checked candidate values, extended for expression tests" /></details>
 <p>The recorded run used UTF-8, database locale <code>C</code>, session <code>TimeZone = UTC</code>, <code>DateStyle = ISO, DMY</code> and <code>standard_conforming_strings = on</code>. Later examples explicitly change a setting and restore it. Check the answering server with <code>SELECT version();</code>; in psql, <code>\pset null 'NULL'</code> makes missing output visible. This fixture uses unconstrained <code>numeric</code> expressions so that the proposed fraction survives until the agreed rounding operation. <a href="/review/relational-next/types/">Choosing representations for stored values</a> owns the storage decision; <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">Enforcing data rules</a> owns enforcement.</p>
 <p>The following calculation runs, but treating every missing number as zero would make 106 look settled. A positive-only follow-up filter would then lose it.</p>
