</section>

<section aria-labelledby="follow-unknown-through-a-predicate">
 <h2 id="follow-unknown-through-a-predicate">Follow unknown through a predicate</h2>
 <p>Order 106’s fee is unknown. So is the answer to “is its fee greater than zero?” Negating that answer still leaves it unknown. <code>NOT (fee &gt; 0)</code> finds known non-positive fees; it does not recover the undecided ones.</p>
 <p>Step through Figure 1, then change <em>Fee policy</em> at the final step. Compare <em>Not positive</em> with <em>Positive or undecided</em>. Watch the predicate’s value separately from WHERE’s decision.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="logic" number={1} />
 <p>The distinction matters because <code>WHERE</code> keeps only true. False and unknown both disappear, although they mean different things. To include undecided fees in a charge review, say <code>fee &gt; 0 OR fee IS NULL</code>. For 106 that is unknown OR true, which is true.</p>
 <table><caption>What a known fact can settle when another predicate is unknown</caption><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Value</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>FALSE AND UNKNOWN</code></td><td>FALSE</td><td>Both cannot be true</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>TRUE AND UNKNOWN</code></td><td>UNKNOWN</td><td>The unknown side still decides</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>TRUE OR UNKNOWN</code></td><td>TRUE</td><td>One true side is sufficient</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>FALSE OR UNKNOWN</code></td><td>UNKNOWN</td><td>The unknown side still decides</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>NOT UNKNOWN</code></td><td>UNKNOWN</td><td>No fact has been added</td></tr>
 </tbody></table>
 <p>Those are truth-value rules, not a left-to-right execution promise. PostgreSQL can reorganise Boolean expressions. An expression that raises an error is not made safe merely by putting a condition before it with AND.</p>
 <p>Another policy is “anything except a known zero”, including credits and undecided fees. <code>fee IS DISTINCT FROM 0</code> gives exactly that: true for a non-zero value or NULL, false for zero. <code>IS NOT DISTINCT FROM</code> is null-safe equality: two missing operands compare equal, one missing operand differs from a known one. Choosing it asserts a comparison policy; it does not discover the missing amounts.</p>
 <Sql code={logic} label="Inspect all predicate values before adding WHERE" />
 <p>Expect the positive-or-undecided policy to select 101 and 106; distinct-from-zero also selects credit 103. Null-sensitive membership is a separate question in <a href="/review/relational-next/query-composition/#define-what-membership-means-when-values-can-be-missing">Composing larger SQL queries</a>. Filtering the missing side of an outer join belongs to <a href="/review/relational-next/joins-and-aggregation/#unmatched">Joining and summarising data</a>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="make-defaults-and-conditional-results-express-the-business-rule">
 <h2 id="make-defaults-and-conditional-results-express-the-business-rule">Make defaults and conditional results express the business rule</h2>
 <p>A searched <code>CASE</code> asks conditions in order and returns the result of the first true one. We need four states here: undecided, waived, credit and charged. Test missingness explicitly with <code>fee IS NULL</code>; the simple form <code>CASE fee WHEN NULL THEN ...</code> would use equality and would not find the null.</p>
 <Sql code={conditional} label="Give missing, zero and negative amounts their own meanings" />
 <p><code>COALESCE</code> returns the first non-null argument. For paid, zero is justified by this stage’s contract. For the order value and proposed fee, it would invent facts. Compare order 106’s two calculation routes in Figure 2, then choose 102, for which both routes appear to work.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="defaults" number={2} />
 <p>The label has a different policy. PostgreSQL treats an empty string as a present value, so <code>COALESCE('', 'Mina')</code> returns the empty string. <code>NULLIF(preferred_label, '')</code> changes precisely an empty label to NULL; COALESCE can then supply the name. A label containing spaces stays spaces under this rule. Trim it only if the input contract says those spaces are insignificant. The query changes its output; it does not update the stored label or invoke a column default.</p>
 <p>CASE results must resolve to one compatible type, as must COALESCE arguments. Keep the numeric amount and its textual state in separate output columns. <code>CASE WHEN fee IS NULL THEN 'undecided' ELSE fee END</code> fails here: PostgreSQL tries to convert the unknown-type string literal to numeric. A textual display requires an explicit conversion, and the numeric value should remain available for calculation.</p>
 <p><code>NULLIF</code> can also make a division defined for zero denominators. The following inspection ratio deliberately returns NULL when there is no non-zero paid amount:</p>
 <Sql code={guards} label="Guard this row's denominator directly" />
 <p>That still needs a business interpretation: NULL means the ratio is unavailable, not zero. PostgreSQL normally skips unneeded CASE arms and COALESCE arguments, but its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-expressions.html#SYNTAX-EXPRESS-EVAL">expression evaluation rules</a> allow earlier work. In our run, an unused CASE arm containing constant <code>1/0</code> still failed during planning. An outer CASE also cannot protect an aggregate whose inputs already raise an error. Guard the operation at the value it consumes; do not rely on clause order or a general promise of short-circuit execution.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="check-the-arithmetic-before-trusting-the-amount">
 <h2 id="check-the-arithmetic-before-trusting-the-amount">Check the arithmetic before trusting the amount</h2>
 <p>The parent introduced integer division. The practical question is where precision disappears. In PostgreSQL, dividing two integers truncates towards zero: <code>5 / 2 = 2</code>, and <code>−5 / 2 = −2</code>. Casting that answer to numeric changes its type, preserving the already truncated value. Convert an operand before division when you require a fractional answer.</p>
 <p>In Figure 3, advance to the operation before its final result. Then switch from division to the rounding example and finally to the large product. Each wrong route makes a decision before the last operation gets a chance.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="arithmetic" number={3} />
 <p>The rounding example discounts two 0.05 lines by 10%. Each unrounded discount is 0.005. Rounding each separately gives 0.01 + 0.01 = 0.02. Adding first and rounding once gives 0.01. Both are valid arithmetic; only the contract tells us which is the payable discount. Formatting the final number as two decimal places cannot settle that policy.</p>
 <p>Our proposed fee of 1.005 similarly remains unrounded until it is added to 101’s 15 due. The agreed final amount is <code>round(16.005::numeric, 2) = 16.01</code>. PostgreSQL numeric rounding sends ties away from zero, so <code>round(−1.005::numeric, 2) = −1.01</code>. Truncation instead gives −1.00. Keep negative credits in the fixture: a rule that agrees on positive values may still treat refunds wrongly.</p>
 <p>An exact decimal calculation can still require an approximation: one divided by three has no finite decimal expansion. Preserve enough intermediate precision for the decision, then round at its agreed boundary. A cast to a fixed scale in the middle is another rounding operation. Inspect <code>pg_typeof(expression)</code> as well as its printed digits when a mixed-type calculation surprises you.</p>
 <Sql code={arithmetic} label="Check operation type, rounding position, zero and a wide intermediate" />
 <p>Two other failures have different causes. On the recorded server, the double-precision expression <code>0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3</code> was false: binary floating-point approximations do not necessarily coincide. A late cast to numeric cannot restore the intended original decimal calculation. And <code>(50000 * 50000)::bigint</code> raised integer overflow before its cast, while <code>50000::bigint * 50000</code> returned 2,500,000,000. The final column’s capacity does not widen an intermediate expression automatically. These are expression decisions; the trade-offs between stored numeric types remain in <a href="/review/relational-next/types/#choose-numeric-representations-from-the-error-you-can-accept">Choosing representations for stored values</a>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="ask-which-text-counts-as-equal-or-matching">
 <h2 id="ask-which-text-counts-as-equal-or-matching">Ask which text counts as equal or matching</h2>
 <p>A customer lookup can mean exact stored spelling, a forgiving name search, or a pattern. Write down which. Under the fixture’s <code>C</code> collation, text José differs from JOSE and from Jose. For the optional forgiving search we explicitly create an ICU collation which ignores case and accents. This requires a PostgreSQL build with ICU support; the recorded server provided it.</p>
 <p>Figure 4 has two name labels and two labels containing digits. First change exact equality to the declared forgiving search. Then compare the unescaped pattern with literal search data <em>100%</em>.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="text" number={4} />
 <p><code>LIKE</code> matches the entire string. A percent matches any sequence, including none; an underscore matches one character. Deliberate outer percents make a contains search. But adding raw input <code>100%</code> between them produces <code>%100%%</code>, which also finds Edda_1000. Escaping the input percent produces <code>%100!%% ESCAPE '!'</code>: the inner escaped percent is literal, and only the outer percents remain wildcards.</p>
 <p>Escape the chosen escape character itself first, then percent and underscore. The executable example does all three with <code>!</code> so that backslash string-literal rules do not obscure the pattern. Parameter binding still belongs in the application; it does not remove LIKE’s wildcard semantics from parameter values.</p>
 <Sql code={text} label="Declare comparison policy and escape a literal contains search" />
 <p>The recorded equality outputs were false, true, false and true. The last two compare precomposed é with e followed by a combining acute accent. Under C they have different bytes; under this nondeterministic ICU collation they compare equal. The collation provider, locale and version are part of the evidence. Do not generalise these examples into a universal Unicode or language rule.</p>
 <p>Operand types matter as well: <code>'Mina'::text = 'Mina '::text</code> was false, whereas comparing those values as <code>char(5)</code> was true because PostgreSQL ignores that type’s trailing padding. C ordering puts ASCII capitals before lowercase letters; it is not a natural-language customer order. Keep the intended collation on the actual comparison or sort expression. A forgiving match also establishes no customer identity: return stable IDs and let the application resolve ambiguous matches. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Performance</a> takes up index support and search cost once the matching policy is correct.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="make-conversions-visible-at-the-expression-boundary">
 <h2 id="make-conversions-visible-at-the-expression-boundary">Make conversions visible at the expression boundary</h2>
 <p>A bare string literal initially has an undetermined type. In <code>20 = '20'</code>, PostgreSQL resolves it as an integer from the comparison context. A value already typed as text is different: <code>'20'::text = 20</code> has no matching operator here. A text column or supplied text value is not guaranteed the same treatment as a literal that merely looks identical.</p>
 <Sql code={parseFailures} label="Conversion failures are different from comparisons returning false" />
 <p>Our proposed-fee import accepts signed fixed-point decimal text with at most three fractional digits. Parsing it as numeric is one step, not the whole validation: PostgreSQL numeric syntax also accepts forms outside that contract. A valid negative amount means a credit here; another field may forbid it. Validate the accepted form and domain before narrowing or rounding it. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> handles parameters, driver conversions and boundary validation.</p>
 <p>The following inputs all parse, but that is insufficient evidence that the result is intended:</p>
 <Sql code={conversion} label="Observe rounding and date parsing rather than guessing from the source text" />
 <table><caption>Observed PostgreSQL 18.6 conversion results</caption><thead><tr><th>Input and operation</th><th>Output</th><th>Decision exposed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>'10.6'::numeric::integer</code></td><td>11</td><td>This conversion rounds</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>trunc('10.6'::numeric)::integer</code></td><td>10</td><td>Discarding the fraction must be explicit</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'03/04/2026'::date</code>, DMY</td><td>3 April</td><td>Day before month</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The same text, MDY</td><td>4 March</td><td>Month before day</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>DATE '2026-04-03'</code></td><td>3 April in both settings</td><td>An explicit type and unambiguous date</td></tr>
 </tbody></table>
 <p>The corresponding negative numeric cast returned −11, whereas truncation returned −10. Conversion can therefore lose information without failing. Use typed, unambiguous literals in the query and an agreed input format at its boundary. Do not write a text-validity WHERE condition followed by a cast and assume the engine will test that condition first: Boolean evaluation order is not guaranteed. If accepting invalid input as an unavailable result is the chosen policy, PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_input_is_valid</code> can test a supported type’s input syntax before a row-dependent CASE cast; it still does not validate our fee’s business rule.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="calculate-reporting-boundaries-in-the-intended-clock">
 <h2 id="calculate-reporting-boundaries-in-the-intended-clock">Calculate reporting boundaries in the intended clock</h2>
 <p>The parent’s January report uses UTC. Suppose the shop instead asks for events on <strong>29 March 2026 in London</strong>. The date identifies a day on a calendar. Its midnight is a local timestamp without an offset. The named zone resolves that reading to an instant, which can be compared with the stored event instants.</p>
 <p>Derive both local midnights separately: 29 March at 00:00 and 30 March at 00:00, each interpreted in Europe/London. In the recorded zone rules these resolve to 29 March 00:00 UTC and 29 March 23:00 UTC. The clock moves forward within the day. The range is 23 hours, with its lower bound included and upper bound excluded.</p>
 <p>Figure 5 compares those boundaries with a fixed 24-hour window. Step to the event comparisons, then choose the autumn day. Every displayed event is an instant in UTC; event IDs here belong to a separate boundary fixture.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="bounds" number={5} />
 <p>A half-open interval makes adjacent reports meet without sharing the boundary event. On the spring day, event 202 exactly at the start belongs; 203 just before the end belongs; 204 exactly at the end and 205 after it belong to tomorrow. Event 201 just before the start belongs to yesterday. Expect <strong>202 and 203</strong>. “Up to 23:59:59” misses fractional seconds, and BETWEEN includes both endpoints.</p>
 <Sql code={boundary} label="Resolve a local calendar range, then compare instants" />
 <p>A fixed offset cannot replace the zone’s rules for both boundaries. London is at +00 at this start and +01 at the end. Choosing the offset in force today for a historical report could select another range. The named zone uses the installed rules for the requested dates; retain the resolved boundaries with important report evidence.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL’s <code>AT TIME ZONE</code> works in two directions. With a <code>timestamp</code> input it interprets a local clock reading in the named zone and returns a timestamptz instant. With a <code>timestamptz</code> input it displays that instant as a local timestamp, without a zone attached. Inspect the input type first. Writing <code>TIMESTAMP</code> alone means without time zone; an offset in its literal text does not turn it into a timestamptz.</p>
 <p>Not every local reading resolves uniquely. London’s <code>2026-03-29 01:30</code> never occurs; <code>2026-10-25 01:30</code> occurs twice. PostgreSQL accepts both inputs using its documented transition rules: the offset before a forward jump and after a backward jump. Both examples below resolved to 01:30 UTC. The spring result displays as 02:30 in London, and the autumn result picks the later occurrence.</p>
 <Sql code={transitions} label="Observe gap and repeated-time resolution, then specify both autumn occurrences" />
 <p>Our report accepts instants for incoming events. A local appointment entry follows a separate policy: reject nonexistent readings and ask the caller to identify which occurrence of a repeated reading they intend. PostgreSQL accepting an ambiguous cast does not implement that policy. In the autumn example, explicit +01 and +00 offsets identify 00:30 and 01:30 UTC respectively. Preserve the required meaning at input; the later report cannot reconstruct a choice that was lost.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="separate-calendar-movement-from-elapsed-duration">
 <h2 id="separate-calendar-movement-from-elapsed-duration">Separate calendar movement from elapsed duration</h2>
 <p>“Move collection to the same local time tomorrow” and “expire this request in 24 hours” ask different questions. At noon in London on 28 March 2026, the former ends at noon the next day after 23 elapsed hours. The latter ends at 13:00 the next day after 24. In autumn the discrepancy reverses.</p>
 <p>Follow both lanes of Figure 6. The session time zone is part of the day-based calculation; switch the starting date to see which clock the hours calculation preserves.</p>
 <ValueFigure scene="calendar" number={6} />
 <Sql code={calendar} label="Compare a calendar-day interval with 24 elapsed hours" />
 <p>In PostgreSQL, an interval retains months, days and elapsed time separately. Adding its day field to timestamptz uses the session zone’s calendar; the hours field adds elapsed time. Set the intended zone deliberately, or use the documented zone argument of <code>date_add</code> when the calculation must name it directly. In UTC the day and 24-hour routes happen to agree across London’s transition.</p>
 <p>Months need their own rule too. The recorded <code>DATE '2026-01-31' + INTERVAL '1 month'</code> returned 28 February at midnight, clamping to the last day of the target month. That is calendar movement, not thirty elapsed days and not necessarily a business rule for “the last day of every month”. For elapsed hours between resolved instants, subtract the instants and use <code>extract(epoch FROM (end_at - start_at)) / 3600</code>; do not subtract their displayed local clock readings.</p>
 <p>A relative period also needs a reference instant. In PostgreSQL, <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> and <code>transaction_timestamp()</code> mean the transaction’s start, <code>statement_timestamp()</code> means the current statement’s start, and <code>clock_timestamp()</code> reads the clock as execution proceeds. Our short two-statement verification kept the transaction time fixed while the statement time advanced.</p>
 <Sql code={clock} label="Observe the three reference clocks inside one transaction" />
 <p>A long-running transaction started yesterday can still call yesterday “current”. For “the last 24 elapsed hours as of this report”, capture one deliberate as-of instant, then use it for every bound, rather than repeatedly reading a moving clock. For “yesterday in London”, derive the calendar date from that instant in London, then resolve that date’s two local midnights as above. The requirements differ even when an ordinary day hides the difference.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="keep-a-small-regression-fixture-for-value-semantics">
 <h2 id="keep-a-small-regression-fixture-for-value-semantics">Keep a small regression fixture for value semantics</h2>
 <p>We can now repair the order follow-up calculation. Only absent receipts receive a numeric default. A missing price or fee keeps the payable unknown; an empty preferred label receives the chosen fallback; rounding happens once at the end. The outer stage keeps positive or unknown payables.</p>
 <Sql code={report} label="The corrected expression report, after the complete fixture setup" />
 <table><caption>Predicted before execution; then observed on native PostgreSQL 18.6</caption><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Label</th><th>Fee state</th><th>Payable</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>101</td><td>Mina</td><td>charged</td><td>16.01</td></tr>
  <tr><td>102</td><td>Mina</td><td>waived</td><td>20.00</td></tr>
  <tr><td>106</td><td>Edda_100%</td><td>undecided</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
 </tbody></table>
 <p>Also inspect the amounts stage without its final filter: 100 must be zero, and 103 must be −2.00. The expected row count alone would not catch a wrong amount or an invented zero. Keep the integer and wide-product checks, ±1.005 rounding, a zero denominator, the two Unicode forms, a label containing literal percent and underscore, invalid numeric input, both DateStyle settings and the time-boundary events. The transition checks also need the two explicit autumn offsets and the spring nonexistent reading.</p>
 <p>Retain the query, predicted values and selected identities beside the observed output, server version and settings. For the ICU example retain the provider, locale and collation version; our run used locale <code>und-u-ks-level1</code>, nondeterministic comparison and ICU collation version <code>153.136</code>. For the time examples retain the named zone and resolved UTC boundaries. Rerun the affected cases after an engine, collation-library, time-zone-data or session-setting change.</p>
 <p>These checks establish the tested values and selections. They do not prove a report’s completeness on untested inputs, or its cost on a large workload. Once the values agree, take the workload and plan questions to <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a>. The fixture’s temporary table disappears when the session closes.</p>
</section>

<section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
 <h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
 <p>Checked on 20 October 2026. The invented order snapshot extends the parent's orders, lines and payments example; it does not use the join sibling's customer-payment schema. SQL outputs were executed on native PostgreSQL 18.6 with the settings recorded above, including the optional ICU comparison. The six interactive figures are explanatory models checked against those outputs, rather than engine traces. No other engine was measured.</p>
 <ul>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-comparison.html">Comparison Functions and Operators</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-logical.html">Logical Operators</a> define null-safe comparisons and the three truth values.</li>
  <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-conditional.html">Conditional Expressions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/typeconv-union-case.html">Type Resolution for CASE and Related Constructs</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-expressions.html#SYNTAX-EXPRESS-EVAL">Expression Evaluation Rules</a>: result types, defaults and limits of conditional guards.</li>
  <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-math.html">Mathematical Functions and Operators</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-numeric.html">Numeric Types</a>: integer division, rounding, approximation and range. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/typeconv-oper.html">Operator Type Resolution</a> explains the unknown literal's inferred type.</li>
  <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/collation.html">Collation Support</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-character.html">Character Types</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-matching.html">Pattern Matching</a>: comparison policies, padding and escaped LIKE patterns.</li>
